with d as (select value r from jsonb_array_elements($q$[{"notes":["This stew tastes better the next day. Make it ahead and reheat gently on the stove.","Dried limes (omani limes) are available at Middle Eastern grocers; if you can't find them, preserved lemon works but use less, the flavour is more intense.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft. If using dried kidney beans, soak them, then boil them hard for at least 10 minutes before simmering, to destroy a natural toxin."],"id":"acbe436b-e221-4634-976d-fc7497b5b1b2"},{"notes":["Leftovers keep, covered, in the fridge for three days and taste even better reheated gently on the stove.","If you can find saucisson de Lyon or a good Lyonnaise sausage, use it; otherwise a quality pork sausage works fine.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the olive oil in a large, heavy-based pot or braising dish over medium heat. Add the diced onion and cook, stirring, until soft and golden, about 5 minutes."},{"text":"Add the chopped garlic and carrot rounds, and cook for another 2 minutes until fragrant."},{"text":"Stir in the lentils, tinned tomatoes, chicken stock, bay leaf and thyme. Bring to a simmer until gently bubbling, then nestle the whole sausages into the lentil mixture."},{"text":"Cover and braise over a low heat for 35–40 minutes, until the lentils are tender and the sausages are cooked through. The sausages should be opaque and firm inside (check by piercing with a knife; juices should run clear)."},{"text":"Remove the bay leaf and thyme sprig. Taste and season with salt and black pepper. Serve the sausages on a bed of lentils, spooning the braising liquid over the top."}],"id":"aac5cc59-8156-4133-a1fa-7eaf0b0f29c8"},{"steps":[{"text":"Make the filling: combine feta, parsley, dill, spring onion, half the beaten egg, salt and pepper in a bowl. Mix gently, you want the cheese to hold together, not become mushy. Taste and adjust seasoning."},{"text":"Set up your workspace: lay one sheet of yufka on a clean bench, long edge closest to you. If the yufka is very large, cut it in half lengthwise to make two strips."},{"text":"Spoon about 2 tbsp filling along the bottom edge, leaving 2 cm on each side. Fold the sides in over the filling, then roll tightly away from you until you have a long, thin, cigarette-shaped roll. Press gently to seal. The seam should be on the bottom."},{"text":"Brush the outside lightly with remaining beaten egg to help it seal and get golden. Repeat with remaining yufka and filling."},{"text":"Heat the oil in a deep heavy-bottomed pan or Dutch oven to 170–180°C. If you don't have a thermometer, test with a small piece of pastry, it should sizzle immediately and brown in about 30 seconds."},{"text":"Working in batches of 3 or 4, fry the börek seam-side down first until they're golden and crispy, about 2 minutes. Turn carefully and fry the other side until evenly golden, another 1–2 minutes."},{"text":"Remove with a slotted spoon and drain on paper towel. Keep warm while you fry the rest."},{"text":"Serve hot, while they're still crispy, with lemon wedges and yoghurt for dipping if you like (though purists like Nonna would serve them plain)."}],"id":"d4b39198-7760-4030-89ce-45c753a8b26f"},{"notes":["Make your own aioli (garlic, mayonnaise, salt) or buy it, the rice and fish are the stars.","Ask the fishmonger for fillets that are thick and skin-on; the crispy skin is essential. Any firm white fish works.","The sofregit can be made ahead and refrigerated; reheat it gently before adding the rice.","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the olive oil in a wide, shallow pan (a paella pan if you have one) over a medium heat. Add the finely chopped onion and cook, stirring often, until soft and golden, about 8 minutes. This is the sofregit, don't rush it, as the sweetness is the soul of the dish."},{"text":"Add the grated tomato to the sofregit and cook, stirring, until the mixture is thick and darkened, about 5 minutes more. The tomato should lose its rawness and merge into the oil and onion."},{"text":"Stir in the rice until well coated in the sofregit, about 1 minute. Pour in the warm seafood stock all at once, add the salt and saffron if using, and bring to a simmer."},{"text":"Simmer, without stirring, for about 18 to 20 minutes, until the rice is tender and has absorbed almost all the stock. The top should look dry, with perhaps a few grains still glistening. Do not stir once the stock is added."},{"text":"While the rice cooks, prepare the fish. Heat the olive oil in a large frying pan or on a grill plate over a medium-high heat until smoking slightly. Season the fish fillets with salt and pepper, then lay them skin-side down in the pan. Cook for 3 to 4 minutes until the skin is golden and crisp, then flip and cook for another 1 to 2 minutes until just cooked through."},{"text":"Divide the rice among four shallow bowls or plates. Top each with a grilled fish fillet and a generous dollop of aioli. Serve immediately."}],"id":"c77a931d-c12d-4dd4-8763-a1ed7af2e8bf"},{"notes":["Prep the vegetables the evening before and store in the fridge, makes baking day much easier.","Undhiyu keeps well and tastes even better the next day. Reheat gently in the oven or microwave.","If banana leaves aren't available, bake covered in parchment paper and foil; it won't have the same flavour but will still be delicious."],"steps":[{"text":"Prep all vegetables: potatoes, sweet potato, eggplant, plantain, pumpkin and green beans. Thinly slice the onion. Have the chickpeas and peanuts ready."},{"text":"Heat 3 tbsp oil in a heavy-bottomed pot or Dutch oven over medium heat. Add cumin seeds and let them crackle, about 1 minute. Add the sliced onion and cook, stirring, until golden, about 8 minutes."},{"text":"Add asafoetida, ground coriander, turmeric and cayenne. Stir constantly for 1 minute until fragrant, being careful not to burn the spices."},{"text":"Add all the prepared vegetables (potato, sweet potato, eggplant, plantain, pumpkin and green beans) and stir to coat with the spiced oil, about 2 minutes."},{"text":"Add the drained chickpeas and roasted peanuts. Stir through."},{"text":"Dissolve the jaggery in 60 ml warm water. Mix in the tamarind paste. Pour this over the vegetables and stir well to combine. Taste and add salt as needed."},{"text":"Line a large baking dish or Dutch oven with banana leaves. Spoon the vegetable mixture into the centre. Fold the banana leaves over to cover, or add more leaves if needed to create a parcel. The leaves will char slightly but won't burn, and the vegetables should be tender when pierced with a knife."},{"text":"Cover the baking dish tightly with foil. Bake at 180°C for 1 hour 15 minutes, until the vegetables are very tender and the flavours have melded into a rich, sweet-spicy-tangy mixture."},{"text":"Unfold the banana leaves carefully (they will be hot), scatter with fresh coriander if you like, and serve warm with rotli, rice or as part of a thali."}],"id":"4d5ee8cd-8cd8-4862-b256-d5376a1cd0c9"},{"diets":["vegetarian","vegan","no-gluten","no-dairy"],"notes":["Make idli within a day or two of preparing your batter, the fresher it is, the fluffier the idli.","If your batter has separated, give it a gentle stir before using. A good batter ferments evenly and keeps in the fridge for up to five days.","No idli mould? Use a muffin tin or small bowls lined with cling wrap, they won't pop out as cleanly, but they'll steam just fine."],"steps":[{"text":"Bring water to a boil in a large pot with an idli stand or steamer rack inside, until it is bubbling steadily and steam is rising. The water should come just below the rack."},{"text":"While the water heats, check your batter. It should fall off a spoon in a thick stream, if it's too stiff, thin it with a little water. Taste and add salt if needed."},{"text":"Oil the idli moulds well (the little metal cups that come with the stand). This matters: the idli pops out cleanly when they're properly greased."},{"text":"Gently pour batter into each mould until three-quarters full. Don't overfill or they'll puff out of shape."},{"text":"Carefully place the mould onto the steamer rack. Steam, covered, until a skewer poked into an idli comes out clean, about 8 minutes."},{"text":"Let rest for 30 seconds, then slide a knife around each idli and turn the mould over onto a plate. They should fall out easily. Serve warm with sambar, chutney or warm oil."}],"id":"3c7306ed-4137-4477-89dd-7aa1b2edcb2e"},{"steps":[{"text":"Heat your cast-iron frying pan in the oven at 200°C for 10 minutes until very hot."},{"text":"Whisk together the cornmeal, plain flour, caster sugar, baking powder and salt in a large bowl."},{"text":"In another bowl, whisk the buttermilk, eggs and melted butter together until well combined."},{"text":"Pour the wet mixture into the dry ingredients and stir until just combined, a few lumps are fine."},{"text":"Carefully remove the hot frying pan from the oven and add a tablespoon of butter, swirling to coat the base and sides."},{"text":"Pour the batter into the hot frying pan and return to the oven. Bake for 20–25 minutes, until golden brown and a skewer inserted in the centre comes out clean."},{"text":"Let cool in the pan for 5 minutes before turning out or serving straight from the frying pan."}],"id":"f83b0559-255c-4965-a010-733474ac9a31"},{"notes":["Make it ahead: cool the sinigang completely, then store in the fridge for up to 3 days. Reheat gently on the stove. The flavours get better the next day, anak.","If you can't find tamarind paste, use 3 tbsp fresh lime juice instead, added at the end.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large pot over medium-high heat. Brown the pork ribs on all sides, about 8 minutes. Remove and set aside."},{"text":"In the same pot, fry the onion and garlic until fragrant, about 2 minutes."},{"text":"Stir in the tamarind paste until well combined, then return the pork ribs to the pot."},{"text":"Pour in the water and bring to a boil. Skim off any scum that rises to the surface. Reduce heat to low, cover, and simmer for 30 minutes until the pork is tender."},{"text":"Add the potatoes and radishes. Simmer uncovered for 10 minutes until starting to soften."},{"text":"Add the okra and leafy greens. Stir gently and cook for another 5 minutes until the greens are wilted and the okra is tender but still bright."},{"text":"Season with salt and pepper to taste. Add the green chilli if using. Taste and adjust the tamarind and salt as needed, it should be sour, savoury and warm."},{"text":"Ladle into bowls and serve hot with rice."}],"id":"c2ef0de1-3c93-4abb-b4e0-9a3f1f5b2f6e"},{"notes":["Make ahead: Prep the chicken and onion earlier in the day, and cook the rice. The broth comes together in 10 minutes flat.","Chicken breast works too, but thigh stays juicier and cooks just as fast.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C. Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the dashi, soy sauce and mirin to a gentle simmer in a large frying pan or shallow pan over medium heat, until small bubbles break the surface."},{"text":"Add the chicken pieces and onion, and simmer for about 5 minutes until the chicken is just cooked through."},{"text":"While the chicken cooks, lightly beat the eggs in a small bowl."},{"text":"When the chicken is done, pour the beaten eggs slowly over the simmering broth in a thin stream, moving the pan gently so the eggs create soft curds. This should take about 1 minute."},{"text":"Let the eggs set for another minute without stirring, they should be creamy and just cooked, not scrambled solid."},{"text":"Divide the warm rice between two bowls and pour the broth, chicken and eggs over the top. Sprinkle with spring onion if using, and serve right away."}],"id":"556c7975-1072-4c42-94db-17fff8d85a42"},{"notes":["Serve with steamed jasmine rice or on its own with a squeeze of lime.","If you can't find laksa paste, use 2 tbsp of any red curry paste as a swap, it won't be quite the same, but it'll still be good.","Makes good leftovers; reheat gently on the stove.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat a large frying pan over medium heat. Add the laksa paste and fry, stirring, for about 2 minutes until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the coconut milk and tamarind paste, stirring to combine. Bring to a gentle simmer until bubbling lightly and the sauce is smooth."},{"text":"Add the chicken pieces and stir to coat in the sauce. Simmer for 15 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the chicken is cooked through."},{"text":"Add the onion wedges and simmer for another 5 minutes until the onion is just tender but still holding its shape."},{"text":"Taste and adjust seasoning with salt. Serve hot with a squeeze of lime and coriander if you like it."}],"id":"91643319-6b7f-480f-932d-eeb70d70d949"},{"notes":["The soaking liquid is flavour, don't skip it.","Once you add stock, don't walk away. Risotto demands your attention for those 18 minutes.","Alba cooks would argue about the exact ratio. Make it how you like it.","Cook chicken until it reaches 75°C in the thickest part and the juices run clear."],"steps":[{"text":"Soak the dried porcini in 200 ml of hot water for 10 minutes. Strain through a fine sieve, keeping the soaking liquid. Roughly chop the porcini."},{"text":"Heat the stock in a separate pot until steaming, and keep it at a gentle simmer throughout cooking."},{"text":"Heat the olive oil in a heavy-bottomed pot over medium heat. Add the diced onion and cook, stirring occasionally, until soft and translucent, about 5 minutes."},{"text":"Add the rice to the onion and stir for 1 minute until lightly toasted. Pour in the white wine and stir until almost completely absorbed."},{"text":"Add the porcini soaking liquid to the rice and stir until absorbed. Start adding the warm stock one ladle at a time, stirring gently, waiting for each ladle to be absorbed before adding the next. This takes about 18 minutes."},{"text":"After about 16 minutes, taste the rice. It should be creamy but with a slight firmness at the centre of each grain. Stir in the chopped porcini."},{"text":"When the rice is almost done and most of the stock is absorbed, remove from heat. Add the butter and parmesan, stirring gently until combined. The risotto should flow slightly on the plate, not stiff, not soup."},{"text":"Season with salt and pepper. Serve immediately."}],"id":"84ce203c-c7f6-4d58-a3d0-ba2ca84e3056"},{"notes":["This soup tastes even better the next day as the flavours deepen. Store in an airtight container in the fridge for up to 3 days; reheat gently.","If you can't find dried scallops, the soup is still good made with just shiitake mushrooms, use 10 or 12 instead.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part. Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."],"steps":[{"text":"Bring a pot of water to a rolling boil. Add the pork ribs and blanch for 2 minutes. Drain and rinse the ribs under cold water until the water runs clear, scrubbing gently to remove impurities. This takes about a minute and makes the soup clean and bright."},{"text":"In a large pot, add 2 litres of fresh water. Bring to a boil, then add the blanched pork ribs, dried shiitake mushrooms, dried scallops, smashed ginger, and half of the spring onion. Return to a boil, then lower the heat until the broth is at a gentle, rolling simmer with small bubbles."},{"text":"Simmer uncovered for 60 minutes. The pork should be almost tender and the broth fragrant. Skim any foam that rises to the surface in the first 15 minutes."},{"text":"Add the winter melon chunks. Bring back to a gentle simmer and cook for another 45 to 60 minutes, until the melon is very soft and translucent and the pork is completely tender. The broth should taste deep and savoury from the long cooking."},{"text":"Taste and season with salt. Discard the ginger and the first batch of spring onion. Serve hot, garnished with the fresh spring onion."}],"id":"7a2eab61-be39-4185-bc49-3d711f955957"},{"notes":["Makes great lunch packet food, it reheats well and tastes even better the next day.","Beef works just as well: use 600g diced beef steak and cook for 10 to 12 minutes until tender.","Store leftovers in an airtight container in the fridge for up to 3 days.","Food safety: Cook chicken and other poultry until the juices run clear and the thickest part reaches 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large frying pan over medium-high heat. Add the mustard seeds and cook, stirring, until they start to pop, about 1 minute."},{"text":"Add the onion and cook, stirring often, until soft and translucent, about 4 minutes."},{"text":"Add the garlic, ginger and green chillies, and cook, stirring, for 1 minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Stir in the cumin and turmeric, cook for another 30 seconds until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the chicken pieces and cook, stirring frequently, until they're cooked through, no pink inside, about 8 to 10 minutes."},{"text":"Pour in the vinegar and salt, and stir through. Taste and add more salt if needed. The dish should have a sharp, spicy edge."},{"text":"Serve hot with rice, roti or hoppers, scattered with fresh coriander if you like."}],"id":"ec736195-ba20-4383-b492-bdf290dedbdb"},{"notes":["If you can't find fresh turmeric noodles, dried ones work fine, cook them a minute or two longer. Some shops sell them as bánh mì quang.","The broth is the hero here: don't skip the shallots and garlic, and don't be stingy with the fish sauce.","Prep the pork and prawns ahead of time, but make the dish fresh when you're ready to eat.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part. Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."],"id":"b4fd4930-f572-4895-8a30-f0df462683f9"},{"notes":["Use only the freshest sashimi-grade fish, ask your fishmonger if it's safe to eat raw.","Don't skip the draining step after the lime cure, or your oka will be too liquid.","Oka is best served straightaway, but you can make it up to 2 hours ahead and keep it cool.","The fish here is eaten raw or only cured in citrus. Buy sashimi-grade fish from a fishmonger, keep it cold and eat it the same day; skip it for anyone pregnant, very young, elderly or with low immunity.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"id":"0b7d3802-6736-4d54-a489-f1ec5082c75a"}]$q$::jsonb)), up as (update public.published_recipes p set recipe = p.recipe || (d.r - 'id' - 'notes') || case when d.r ? 'notes' then jsonb_build_object('notes', coalesce((select jsonb_agg(n) from jsonb_array_elements(d.r->'notes') n where btrim(n#>>'{}') <> 'Food safety:'),'[]'::jsonb)) else '{}'::jsonb end, diets = case when d.r ? 'diets' then array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(d.r->'diets')) else p.diets end from d where p.id=(d.r->>'id')::uuid returning p.id) select (select count(*) from d) expected, (select count(*) from up) updated;